    "other-rental-car-waiver@01": {
      "ask": "Before you decline anything at the counter, find out what would pay if the rental were damaged or stolen. If you plan to rely on a credit card, ask its issuer for the rental coverage terms in writing, including whether it pays first or second. If nothing else covers the rental, the waiver may be worth buying for that trip.",
      "background": "The extra coverage a rental company sells at the counter, called a collision or loss damage waiver, is not technically insurance. It relieves you of responsibility if the rental car is damaged or stolen. You did not tell us about a car insurance policy, so there may be no policy of your own for a rental to fall back on. That makes it more important to check what else would pay before you decline anything. Many credit cards cover rental car damage when you pay for the rental with the card, but you generally have to decline the rental company's waiver to use it. That coverage is usually secondary, meaning it pays only after your own car insurance or the rental company's coverage, and it may cover only your deductible. If nothing else covers the rental and you decline the waiver, you can owe for the damage, sometimes up to the car's full value. If you carry a credit card, check its current benefits guide for rental car coverage before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.",
      "bucket": "question",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "I want to know what would pay if a rental car were damaged before I decide whether the rental company's coverage is worth buying.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-rental-car-insurance",
        "ftc-renting-a-car",
        "naic-consumer-auto-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Find out what would pay for a rental car before you buy the extra coverage"
    },
    "other-rental-car-waiver@02": {
      "ask": "Before your next rental, ask your car insurer whether your policy covers a rental car on personal trips, including theft and damage, and what your deductible would be. Rules vary, so get the answer from your insurer or your state's department of insurance. Then, if you plan to rely on a card, ask its issuer for the rental terms in writing: whether it pays first or second, and what you must do to qualify, which usually means paying with the card and declining the waiver. If both cover you, decline the waiver at the counter.",
      "background": "The extra coverage a rental company sells at the counter, called a collision or loss damage waiver, is not technically insurance. It relieves you of responsibility if the rental car is damaged or stolen. You may already have that protection. If you still carry collision and comprehensive on your own car (collision pays for crash damage, comprehensive for things like theft and hail), the coverage and deductible on your car generally apply to a rental you use for personal trips. Many credit cards cover rental car damage when you pay for the rental with the card, but you generally have to decline the rental company's waiver to use it. That coverage is usually secondary, meaning it pays only after your own car insurance or the rental company's coverage, and it may cover only your deductible. If nothing else covers the rental and you decline the waiver, you can owe for the damage, sometimes up to the car's full value. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes rental car coverage before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.",
      "bucket": "drop",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "I want to confirm my own policy and my card already cover a rental car before I pay the rental company for the same protection.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-rental-car-insurance",
        "ftc-renting-a-car",
        "naic-consumer-auto-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Check whether you can skip the rental company's extra coverage"
    },
    "other-rental-car-waiver@03": {
      "ask": "Ask your car insurer whether anything on your policy extends to a rental car, and what you would owe if one were damaged or stolen. If you plan to rely on a credit card, ask its issuer for the rental coverage terms in writing, including whether it pays first or second. If nothing else covers the rental, the waiver may be worth buying for that trip.",
      "background": "The extra coverage a rental company sells at the counter, called a collision or loss damage waiver, is not technically insurance. It relieves you of responsibility if the rental car is damaged or stolen. You told us you do not carry collision and comprehensive on your car. Without them, your own policy may not pay if a rental car is damaged or stolen, so the waiver, or a card that covers rentals, may be the only thing standing between you and the repair bill. Many credit cards cover rental car damage when you pay for the rental with the card, but you generally have to decline the rental company's waiver to use it. That coverage is usually secondary, meaning it pays only after your own car insurance or the rental company's coverage, and it may cover only your deductible. If nothing else covers the rental and you decline the waiver, you can owe for the damage, sometimes up to the car's full value. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes rental car coverage before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.",
      "bucket": "question",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "I want to know what would pay if a rental car were damaged before I decide whether the rental company's coverage is worth buying.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-rental-car-insurance",
        "ftc-renting-a-car",
        "naic-consumer-auto-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Find out what would pay for a rental car before you buy the extra coverage"
    },
