    "renters-child-vs-parents-home@01": {
      "ask": "Ask your home insurer whether your policy covers your child's belongings in their dorm, up to how much, and what conditions apply, such as their age and whether they are enrolled in classes. Also ask whether anything especially valuable your child owns has a lower limit or needs a special add-on, called a floater. Then compare the answers with what your child's renters policy pays, and ask the renters insurer what it adds beyond your home policy before you decide whether to keep it.",
      "background": "A student who lives in a dorm and is still part of your household is generally covered by your home policy's off-premises coverage, which protects belongings away from home. That means the renters policy you pay for may be doing a job your home policy already does. The details vary by insurer. Your policy may pay only up to a set amount for belongings away from home, it may set conditions such as your child's age and whether they are enrolled in classes, and your deductible (what you pay first on any claim) still applies. A renters policy can still make sense if your child's belongings are worth more than your home policy would pay, so compare before you decide.",
      "bucket": "question",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "My child lives in a dorm, so I want to know whether my home policy already covers their belongings and what the renters policy adds.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-college-student-losses",
        "naic-renters-college-2024",
        "naic-renters-college-students",
        "iii-am-i-covered",
        "iii-renters-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Check whether your home policy already covers your child's belongings"
    },
    "renters-child-vs-parents-home@02": {
      "ask": "Ask your home insurer whether your policy reaches a place your child rents off campus; it likely does not. If it does not, your child probably needs their own renters policy, so ask the renters insurer whether its limit is enough to replace what your child owns and what the deductible is.",
      "background": "A student who lives off campus is likely not covered by your home policy, and some insurers may not extend student coverage to an apartment rented in the student's name. So the renters policy you pay for is probably not a duplicate. The building owner's insurance and the school's insurance do not cover your child's personal items either, so without their own policy your child would probably have nothing to fall back on.",
      "bucket": "question",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "My child rents off campus, so I want to confirm my home policy does not reach them and that their renters policy limit is enough.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-college-student-losses",
        "naic-renters-college-2024",
        "naic-renters-college-students",
        "iii-am-i-covered",
        "iii-renters-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Confirm your child's renters policy covers what they own"
    },
    "renters-child-vs-parents-home@03": {
      "ask": "Ask your home insurer whether your policy covers your child's belongings where they live now, up to how much, and what conditions apply, such as their age and whether they are enrolled in classes. Then compare the answers with what your child's renters policy pays, and ask the renters insurer what it adds beyond your home policy.",
      "background": "Whether your home policy reaches a child's belongings depends on where they live. A student in a dorm who is still part of your household is generally covered by your home policy's off-premises coverage (the part that protects belongings away from home), which means a separate renters policy may be a duplicate. A student who lives off campus is likely not covered, and probably needs their own renters policy. Either way the details vary by insurer. Your policy may pay only up to a set amount for belongings away from home, and your deductible (what you pay first on any claim) still applies.",
      "bucket": "question",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "I want to know whether my home policy covers my child's belongings where they live, and what a separate renters policy adds.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-college-student-losses",
        "naic-renters-college-2024",
        "naic-renters-college-students",
        "iii-am-i-covered",
        "iii-renters-insurance"
      ],
      "title": "Check whether your home policy already covers your child's belongings"
    },
    "umbrella-auto-below-required-min@01": {
      "ask": "Ask your agent: \"What would it cost to raise my auto liability to 250/500?\" and \"Until I do, will my umbrella still respond the way I expect if I cause a serious accident?\" Then raise the limit to at least 250/500 and keep the answers in writing.",
      "background": "Auto liability limits are usually written as three numbers, like 250/500/100: the most for one person you hurt, the most for everyone you hurt in one accident, and the most for property damage, in thousands of dollars. This tool asks only about the first two. An umbrella is extra liability coverage that starts paying only after those limits are used up, and umbrella insurers set a minimum for what has to be underneath. You told us yours asks for 250/500 and your auto policy has 100/300. As a rough yardstick, the Insurance Information Institute says most insurers want about $250,000 of auto liability under a $1 million umbrella, but it does not say how that number is split and each insurer sets its own. We found no published rule for what happens when the limit underneath is below that minimum, so the safe move is to close the gap rather than find out during a claim.",
      "bucket": "raise",
      "emphasis": "normal",
      "reason": "My umbrella asks for 250/500 of liability on my auto policy and mine is 100/300, so I want it raised to match.",
      "sources": [
        "iii-should-i-purchase-umbrella",
        "iii-what-is-umbrella-liability-policy",
        "naic-auto-shopping-tool"
      ],
      "title": "Raise your auto liability limit to what your umbrella asks for"
    },
