      "background": "An umbrella is a separate policy that adds liability coverage (the part that pays when you are legally responsible for hurting someone or damaging their property) on top of your home and auto policies. It starts paying once the liability limit underneath it is used up. A simple test from the Insurance Information Institute (Triple-I): do you hold property, investments or savings worth more than your liability limits? You told us you have a $1 million umbrella and could stand to lose more than $3 million. Your umbrella is smaller than everything in that range. That range is a rough guide, not a prediction of what a court would award.",
